CAxisTensor -- the signature tensor of the canonical axis path at a given level
*******************************************************************************

  * Usage: 
        s = CAxisTensor(k, R)
  * Inputs:
      * k, an *note integer: (Macaulay2Doc)ZZ,, the level of the signature
        tensor to compute;
      * R, an instance of the type *note NCPolynomialRing:
        (NCAlgebra)NCPolynomialRing,, the output tensor space;
  * Outputs:
      * s, an instance of the type *note NCRingElement:
        (NCAlgebra)NCRingElement,, an element of R; the k-th level signature of
        the canonical axis path in $\mathbb R^d$, where $d$ is the number of
        generators of R;

Description
===========

The canonical axis path in $\mathbb{R}^d$ is the path from $(0, \dots, 0)$ to
$(1, \dots, 1)$ given by $d$ linear steps in the unit directions $e_1, \dots,
e_d$, in this order. The $k$-th level signature tensor of such a path has a
combinatorial closed-form description (see the reference below) and can be
obtained as follows:

+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i1 : d = 2;                                                                  |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i2 : k = 3;                                                                  |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i3 : R = wordAlgebra(d);                                                     |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i4 : Cd = CAxisTensor(k, R); Cd // wordFormat -- k-th level signature of the |
|                                                                             |
|     1             1             1             1                             |
|o5 = - [2, 2, 2] + - [1, 2, 2] + - [1, 1, 2] + - [1, 1, 1]                   |
|     6             2             2             6                             |
|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------|
|canonical axis path in R^d                                                   |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+

To expand on the example, we verify that the result agrees with the one
obtained from *note sig: sig,. Notice that the matrix of increments for the
canonical axis path in dimension $d$ is the $d \times d$ identity matrix.

+---------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i6 : M = id_(QQ^d); -- identity matrix                                     |
|                                                                           |
|              2       2                                                    |
|o6 : Matrix QQ  <-- QQ                                                     |
+---------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i7 : CAxisPath = pwLinPath(M) -- the canonical axis path in dimension d    |
|                                                                           |
|o7 = Path in 2-dimensional space with 2 polynomial segments:               |
|                                                                           |
|     {{t, 0}, {0, t}}                                                      |
|                                                                           |
|o7 : Path                                                                  |
+---------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i8 : Cd2 = sig(CAxisPath, k); Cd2 // wordFormat -- the k-th level signature|
|                                                                           |
|     1             1             1             1                           |
|o9 = - [2, 2, 2] + - [1, 2, 2] + - [1, 1, 2] + - [1, 1, 1]                 |
|     6             2             2             6                           |
+---------------------------------------------------------------------------+

References
==========

Varieties Of Signature Tensors (doi.org/10.1017/fms.2019.3) (see
https://doi.org/10.1017/fms.2019.3 )



Ways to use CAxisTensor:
========================

  * "CAxisTensor(ZZ,NCPolynomialRing)"

For the programmer
==================

The object *note CAxisTensor: CAxisTensor, is a *note method function:
(Macaulay2Doc)MethodFunction,.

